## 10. Comparing Multiple Runs ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

We created two runs with different training configurations.

MLflow allowed us to compare:

```text
RUN 1                 RUN 2

Parameters            Parameters
Accuracy              Accuracy
Precision             Precision
Recall                Recall
F1                    F1
Artifacts             Artifacts
```

We also explored the **Parallel Coordinates Plot** in the MLflow UI.

It visually connects parameter configurations and metrics across selected runs.

### Important ML lesson

The model with the highest accuracy is not necessarily the best model for production.

For our SRE Incident Predictor:

```text
Run A
Accuracy = 95%
Recall   = 50%

Run B
Accuracy = 90%
Recall   = 95%
```

Run B may be more suitable when missing real incidents is particularly costly.

However, we must also consider precision, false-positive volume, data quality and evaluation reliability.

**MLflow records results. It does not automatically determine which model is operationally best.**

## 11. Run ID vs Run Name

Every MLflow run has a unique Run ID.

MLflow may also assign a readable run name.

Example:

```text
Run ID:
Unique identifier used to reference a run

Run Name:
Human-readable label for easier navigation
```

Run names can be customized.

Run IDs are useful for tracing artifacts and model lineage.

## 12. MLflow Tracking vs Model Registry

Do not confuse the two.

**Experiment Tracking:**

```text
What did we try?
Which parameters?
Which metrics?
Which artifacts?
Which run?
```

**Model Registry:**

```text
Which models are registered?
Which versions exist?
Which model is champion?
Which candidate is approved?
```

Model Registry is covered separately in **Day 3**.

## 13. What MLflow Does NOT Do Automatically

MLflow Tracking does not automatically:

- Make an ML model accurate.
- Guarantee reproducible training.
- Validate the quality of our dataset.
- Choose the best model for production.
- Deploy a model simply because it was logged.
- Guarantee that every relevant dependency or dataset version was captured.

We still need appropriate training code, data lineage, versioning, evaluation and deployment processes.

MLflow provides infrastructure for tracking and organizing those details.

## 14. Common Mistakes

### Mistake 1 — Confusing Experiment and Run

Incorrect mental model:

```text
Experiment = one training execution
```

Correct:

```text
Experiment
   ├── Run 1
   ├── Run 2
   └── Run 3
```

### Mistake 2 — Confusing Parameters and Metrics

```text
max_iter = 1000
```

Parameter.

```text
recall = 0.75
```

Metric.

### Mistake 3 — Thinking Every Artifact Is a Metric

```text
evaluation.json
```

Artifact.

```text
accuracy = 0.91
```

Metric.

### Mistake 4 — Assuming Logging Means Deployment

```text
Train
  ↓
Log Model
  ↓
Tracked Model
```

This does not automatically mean the model is serving production traffic.

### Mistake 5 — Comparing Runs Only by Accuracy

For incident prediction, recall and false negatives may be more important than overall accuracy.

## 15. MLOps / SRE Connection

Our original workflow:

```text
Train
  ↓
Evaluate
  ↓
Print Results
  ↓
Save Model
```

Now becomes:

```text
Train
  ↓
Evaluate
  ↓
MLflow Run
  ├── Parameters
  ├── Metrics
  ├── Tags
  ├── Artifacts
  └── Logged Model
       ↓
MLflow UI
       ↓
Compare Runs
       ↓
Investigate Model Behavior
```

This is our first step toward a traceable, managed ML lifecycle.

## 16. Quick Revision Cheat Sheet

```python
import mlflow

# Set tracking destination
mlflow.set_tracking_uri(
    "http://127.0.0.1:5000"
)

# Select experiment
mlflow.set_experiment(
    "sre-incident-predictor"
)

# Start tracked run
with mlflow.start_run():

    # Parameters
    mlflow.log_param("max_iter", 1000)

    # Metrics
    mlflow.log_metric("accuracy", 0.91)
    mlflow.log_metric("recall", 0.85)

    # Tags
    mlflow.set_tag("purpose", "baseline")

    # Artifact
    mlflow.log_artifact("evaluation.json")

    # Model (assuming model is already fitted)
    mlflow.sklearn.log_model(
        sk_model=model,
        name="incident_model"
    )
```

